1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,080
The problem isn't on the frontend.

2
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:01,720
It's not the backend.

3
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:02,340
Not the network. It's Redis.

4
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:02,940
It's in Redis.

5
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:04,320
Impossible. I set up the Redis cluster myself.

6
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,920
A three-master, three-slave sentinel setup. It's rock-solid.

7
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,500
Sorry to drag you all out here in the middle of the night.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:24,140
And you know why, don't you?

9
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:25,140
Alright, who wants to throw the first stone?

10
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:26,060
Well, it's not a backend issue anyway.

11
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,100
I checked the service status before I went to bed, and the logs were clean.

12
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:29,080
And it's not my front-end either.

13
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:30,300
What about our CDA cache?

14
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:31,940
The cache hit rate has been consistently above 98%.

15
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:33,040
Then it's a problem with the network layer.

16
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,880
Zihao, that's on you. What's it got to do with me?

17
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,440
I just inspected the network yesterday. The bandwidth utilization is only at 40%.

18
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:40,280
What's the problem?

19
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:41,740
Who are you calling a spaghetti code monkey?

20
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:43,340
Well, it's not my front-end that's screwed up.

21
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,090


22
00:00:45,090 --> 00:00:46,490
My pre-launch test report...

23
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:49,470
Page 17 clearly states the risk of cache penetration in high-throughput scenarios.

24
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:50,790
I recommend we optimize the hot key strategy.

25
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:52,190
You all signed off on it, but you didn't make the changes.

26
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:52,960
And I have proof.

27
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,680
So, nobody knows what the problem is, and it's already been 61 minutes.

28
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:55,360
36,000 users are now getting a blank screen when they open the app.

29
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:56,620
Right now, opening the app just gives you a blank white screen.

30
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:57,320
You guys just keep arguing.

31
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:58,100
And they kept arguing until 8 AM.

32
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,800
The first thing the Ops team does when they get in is send a company-wide email.

33
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:01,660
Subject line: Tech team blames each other in the middle of the night.

34
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:04,190
Does 'user churn' sound good to you?

35
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:05,450
What are you doing here?

36
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:06,630
Besides, Product Managers don't have to be on call.

37
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:08,030
What does a PM like you know about tech?

38
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:09,280
I was awake at 12.

39
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:10,600
The first message in the alert group was at 12:47.

40
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,960
It was sent by Zihao's automated alert bot.

41
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:13,760
And you guys didn't get here until 3.

42
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:15,740
I've been going through the logs for the past two hours.

43
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:17,860
So, what have you found?

44
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,020
The problem isn't on the front end.

45
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:20,580
And not on the backend either.

46
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:21,180
It's not the network. It's Redis.

47
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:22,980
No way! I set up the Redis cluster myself.

48
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:25,560
A three-master, three-slave Sentinel setup. It's rock-solid.

49
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:26,200
Mr. Haoyu.

50
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,240
Hold on, let's not jump to conclusions.

51
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:28,280
Look at this line.

52
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:31,420
Sometime between 11 PM and midnight tonight.

53
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:35,460
Redis's memory usage mysteriously spiked from 62% to 98%.

54
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,820
Then, at 12:43 AM,

55
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,580
The master node auto-restarted due to OOM.

56
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:42,320
After the restart, the entire cache was wiped out.

57
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:43,830
The entire cache was wiped clean.

58
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:44,850
30,000 user requests at the same time...

59
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:46,930
In a millisecond, they went straight through to the MySQL database.

60
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:48,030
It can't handle this at all. The cache is in a complete avalanche.

61
00:01:48,030 --> 00:01:51,820
Exactly. But the real culprit behind this avalanche isn't Redis's configuration.

62
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:53,820
It was that marketing campaign that went live at 8 PM tonight.

63
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:54,580
What marketing campaign?

64
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:56,700
It was the pre-Qingming Festival campaign. The ops team sent out a push notification at 8 PM.

65
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,100
The open rate was a full three times higher than expected.

66
00:01:58,100 --> 00:01:59,860
A massive influx of new users started frantically requesting cold data.

67
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:01,540
But the problem was, those keys didn't even exist in Redis.

68
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:03,300
The cache miss rate skyrocketed from 2% to 35%.

69
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:04,340
MySQL was instantly overwhelmed.

70
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:05,300
Then, Redis's memory was completely maxed out.

71
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:05,940
The OAD restart triggered a secondary avalanche.

72
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:06,780
triggering a secondary avalanche.

73
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:08,300
It was a perfect, cascading collapse.

74
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,639
So it has absolutely nothing to do with my front-end. I'm clean.

75
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:12,200
But that wasn't even the worst part.

76
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:13,240
The real problem was...

77
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:14,720
We could have stopped all of this.

78
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,120
We didn't have a cache pre-warming mechanism.

79
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:17,120
Before the new campaign went live.

80
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:19,440
We should have preloaded the hot data into Redis during the pre-launch check.

81
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,030
It was clearly stated in item 4 of the meeting minutes.

82
00:02:21,030 --> 00:02:22,830
I suggest we add a cache pre-warming script.

83
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:24,610
It should run automatically 30 minutes before the event goes live.

84
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:27,060
The conclusion at the time was to push it to phase two.

85
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,160
The reason was that we didn't have any caching issues at the time.

86
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:30,380
It wasn't a high priority.

87
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:39,610
And the person who signed off on that decision... was you, Haoyu. Alright, we know the reason now.

88
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:41,050
So, how do we fix it now?

89
00:02:41,050 --> 00:02:42,330
The fastest solution.

90
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:42,730
I've already drafted a three-step plan.

91
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:43,290
And there's an owner for each step.

92
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:43,810
And I've got an estimated time for each.

93
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:44,970
First step: don't use the default configuration.

94
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:47,980
Change the maxmemory-policy to allkeys-lfu, and enable lazy-free on expire and on eviction.

95
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:49,420
This will speed up memory reclamation after a restart.

96
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:50,960
This should prevent another OOM. ETA is 5 minutes.

97
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:52,720
You're a product manager, and you're reading the Redis docs?

98
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,040
Step two, Haoyu, add a token bucket rate limiter to the MySQL access layer.

99
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:55,880
Set the QPS limit to 3,000.

100
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:57,360
And at the same time, temporarily degrade the 7 interfaces related to slow queries.

101
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:58,160
Huaihui, you're on fallback data.

102
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:58,760
Prioritize the red ones.

103
00:02:58,760 --> 00:02:59,360
The yellow ones are next.

104
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:00,660
Estimated time: 15 minutes.

105
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:01,660
He even factored in the rate limiting maintenance.

106
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:02,300
Where did the three thousand come from?

107
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:03,860
So, for MySQL, without any caching or a safety net...

108
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:04,980
The safe QPS for a single machine is 4,000.

109
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:05,260
So, we take 20% off.

110
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:06,900
So, a 20% buffer brings it down to 3,200. Then, round it down to the nearest thousand, which is 3,000.

111
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:07,500
I've run the numbers three different ways.

112
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,130
This is the most conservative approach.

113
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:12,710
Step three, Zihan. Front-end, add a loading fallback page and an exponential backoff for the 'Rich 10' feature.

114
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:14,510
Change the white screen to 'System Busy'.

115
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:15,910
ETA for recovery is 10 minutes.

116
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:16,750
For the exponential backoff, start with a 3-second interval.

117
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:17,990
Double the interval each time, with some random jitter.

118
00:03:17,990 --> 00:03:20,209
This prevents all users from retrying at the exact same second, which would overwhelm the backend.

119
00:03:20,209 --> 00:03:21,669
3 seconds, 6 seconds, 12 seconds.

120
00:03:21,669 --> 00:03:23,349
Jitter range is plus or minus 30%.

121
00:03:23,349 --> 00:03:24,789
I know you well, but what about me?

122
00:03:24,789 --> 00:03:25,489
Mr. Haoxuan.

123
00:03:25,489 --> 00:03:26,369
You're on recovery metrics.

124
00:03:26,369 --> 00:03:28,000
I've put together a checklist for you.

125
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,920
There are three metrics. First, get the error rate down to below 5%.

126
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,160
P99 latency back under 200 milliseconds.

127
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:32,800
Only then can we say we're back in business.

128
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:33,800
Report the numbers in the group chat every five minutes.

129
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:35,560
Got it. When did you even write this plan?

130
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:37,600
Writing a failure预案 on the day of the launch.

131
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:38,480
So you don't write a预案 on launch day?

132
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:39,020
When did you write it?

133
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:39,760
And that's not all.

134
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,660
This is the failover plan for the master-slave database in case the switch fails.

135
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:43,260
This one's the contingency plan for CDN node failures.

136
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:44,720
This is the contingency plan for payment gateway reconciliation.

137
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,320
This one's for third-party API timeouts.

138
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:47,220


139
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:49,200
And the third one is the one we're using today.

140
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:51,820
But weren't you just sitting at your desk the whole afternoon on launch day?

141
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:52,940
I thought you were just slacking off.

142
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:54,800
I was writing seven different contingency plans.

143
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,350
Error rate's down from 15%, and dropping.

144
00:03:57,350 --> 00:04:00,210
All three metrics are green.

145
00:04:01,330 --> 00:04:03,110
From identification to recovery: 64 minutes.

146
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:05,470
The recovery time we estimated in the预案 was 60 minutes.

147
00:04:05,470 --> 00:04:06,310
Four minutes over.

148
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:08,500
Because Haoyu misspelled an interface name in the middle of it all.

149
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:09,180
I had to change it twice.

150
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,500
The interface name you wrote in your proposal was different from the one I actually used.

151
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:12,420
Yeah, that was my bad.

152
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:14,440
I'll double-check your interface docs with you beforehand next time.

153
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:15,780
By the time you get the update out...

154
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:17,899
He can even recite the Redis configurations and parameters from memory.

155
00:04:17,899 --> 00:04:19,760
Even with 8 years of Ops experience, I still have to look up the docs.

156
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,620
Seven different预案s.

157
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:23,480
Seven of them? I can't even be bothered to write a single RDBMS plan.

158
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:24,870
Guys, don't you think he's...

159
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:28,110
He's becoming more and more like us.

160
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:29,790
No, he's better than us.

161
00:04:29,790 --> 00:04:30,570
We write the code.

162
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:31,310
He writes contingency plans.

163
00:04:31,310 --> 00:04:32,310
We fix bugs.

164
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:33,310
He prevents bugs.

165
00:04:33,310 --> 00:04:34,280
We cure the symptoms.

166
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:35,960
He prevents the illness. From now on, for all requirement reviews...

167
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:37,120
He said it's not that we didn't have a technical solution.

168
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:38,320
I was honestly too scared to say anything.

169
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:39,280
Because he actually knows his stuff.

170
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:41,650
I should've listened to him before going live.

171
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:43,690
and get the cache preheating done.

172
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:46,810
What do you guys think would happen if he actually learned how to code?

173
00:04:46,810 --> 00:04:48,640
No way.

174
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:51,220
But what if?

